WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.520
"Can Alzheimer's Disease Be Reversed
with a Plant Based Diet?"

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:10.040
Dr. Dean Ornish was
the first to show,

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:12.400
in a randomized
controlled trial,

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:15.040
that a plant-based diet
and lifestyle program

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:18.000
could apparently reverse
the progression

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.120
of our number one killer,
heart disease.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:24.520
Opening up arteries without
drugs, without surgery.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:27.640
Then, he showed the same
plant-based program

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:29.240
could potentially
reverse the course

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:31.840
of early-stage
prostate cancer

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:34.000
and also elongate
telomeres,

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.640
suggesting an anti-
aging effect as well.

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:40.280
But when he told me
he was going to see

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:43.600
if he could reverse the progression
of Alzheimer's disease,

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:45.480
I was skeptical.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:50.840
Surely, he was biting off a little
more than he could chew.

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:54.640
Dementia is the most
feared condition of later life.

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:57.040
There’s a common
misconception

00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:58.720
that we have no control

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:01.120
over whether we
develop dementia,

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:03.480
but the good news is

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:06.994
that although Alzheimer’s
may be incurable,

00:01:06.994 --> 00:01:10.800
at least it is preventable.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:12.880
There is an emerging consensus

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:17.800
that “what’s good for our hearts
is also good for our heads,”

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:21.680
because clogging of the
arteries inside the brain

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:25.400
with atherosclerotic plaque
is thought to play a role

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:28.560
in the development of
Alzheimer’s dementia.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:33.960
This is what our cerebral
arteries should look like:

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:37.120
open, clean, and
allowing blood to flow

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:39.800
throughout our brain.

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:45.560
This is what atherosclerosis
in our head looks like:

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:48.160
clogged with cholesterol,
closing off our arteries,

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:50.840
and clamping down
on blood flow.

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:56.400
What kind of brain arteries do
you want in your head?

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:58.360
Too much cholesterol
in our blood

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:02.200
is unanimously recognized
to be a risk factor

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:05.560
for the development
of Alzheimer’s disease.

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:08.880
Those with a total cholesterol
of 225 mg/dL or more

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:12.040
may have nearly
25 times the odds

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:14.560
of ending up with amyloid
plaques in their brain

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:17.160
10 to 15 years later.

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:20.800
After all, what is the
Alzheimer’s gene, APOE?

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:26.280
It codes for the major cholesterol
carrier inside the brain.

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:30.120
This may explain the
so-called Nigerian paradox:

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:33.920
They have among the highest
rates of the Alzheimer’s gene

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:37.400
but some of the lowest
rates of Alzheimer’s disease.

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:38.960
How is that possible?

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:43.160
Genes load the gun,
but lifestyle pulls the trigger.

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:46.960
The paradox may be explained
by their low cholesterol levels,

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:50.520
probably due to their
diets low in animal fat.

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:56.640
So, in terms of dietary guidelines
for the prevention of Alzheimer’s,

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:59.400
we should center our
diets around vegetables,

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:04.800
legumes, fruits,
and whole grains.

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:08.760
In other words, the dietary
pillar of lifestyle medicine:

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:11.560
whole food,
plant-based nutrition.

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:18.360
Or, if that’s too complicated,
"plants, plants, and more plants."

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:20.960
That may help explain
why vegetarians

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:26.240
may be up to three times less likely
to become demented later in life.

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:28.280
But it’s not all-or-nothing.

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:34.080
Even just substituting 5%
of animal protein with plant protein

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:39.600
appears to significantly reduce
the risk of dying from dementia.

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:42.760
But prevention isn’t sexy.

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:46.920
When prevention works,
nothing happens,

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:50.640
but the same diet and lifestyle
that helps prevent heart disease

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:53.240
was proven to
help reverse it.

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:56.280
Until then, it was believed
that heart disease progression

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:59.720
could only be slowed,
not stopped or reversed,

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:04.800
similar to how Alzheimer’s
disease is viewed today.

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:07.600
So, what if you put
people with Alzheimer’s

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:09.760
on the same plant-
based program?

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:15.280
You don’t know until
you put it to the test.

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:16.840
A randomized,
controlled,

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:18.760
phase 2 clinical trial

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:21.400
to see if the progression
of Alzheimer’s disease

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:25.360
may be slowed, stopped,
or perhaps even reversed.

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:28.080
They randomized about
50 men and women

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:30.960
diagnosed with early-
stage Alzheimer’s

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:34.560
to either make no lifestyle
changes for 20 weeks

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:38.600
or eat a whole food,
plant-based diet

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:41.160
(with supplements
like vitamin B12),

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:42.920
moderate exercise

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:45.280
(like walking half
an hour a day),

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:47.240
stress management

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:50.000
(like relaxing with
breathing exercises),

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:54.360
and getting group
support (over Zoom).

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:56.120
They measured
standard tests

00:04:56.120 --> 00:04:59.400
of cognition and function
before and after in each group,

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:02.120
as well as objective
experimental biomarkers

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:04.040
of disease progression.

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:07.040
On the Clinical Dementia
Rating Global scale,

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:10.880
which is used to stage
the severity of dementia,

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:14.960
the control group
continued to get worse,

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:19.720
but the diet and lifestyle
group started to get better.

00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:24.080
People diagnosed with
Alzheimer’s getting better?

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:26.360
The same seemed to
happen when measured

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:29.440
with the Alzheimer’s Disease
Assessment Scale,

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:33.360
though this did not reach
statistical significance.

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:34.570
Using what’s called the

00:05:34.570 --> 00:05:37.400
Clinical Dementia Rating
Sum of Boxes scoring,

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:40.360
both groups
continued to deteriorate,

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:45.320
but the decline was significantly
less in the healthy living group.

00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:47.360
Overall, using
what’s called the

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:50.800
Clinical Global Impression
of Change scoring,

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:54.840
most of the people in the
control group kept getting worse

00:05:54.840 --> 00:06:00.720
and none showed any improvement,
which is what you'd expect with Alzheimer's,

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:05.360
whereas about 40% of those
in the diet and lifestyle group

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:08.840
appeared to be getting
better within five months

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:11.571
of eating and
living healthier.

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:14.360
Why did some get
better and others not?

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:17.240
Well, the more they complied
with the recommendations,

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:21.680
the greater the beneficial impact
on their cognition and function.

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:23.240
This helps to
explain why studies

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:25.880
of less-intensive
lifestyle interventions

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:29.600
were not sufficient to
stop disease progression,

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:33.240
let alone actually improve
cognition and function.

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:36.920
The biggest limitation
of the study is that,

00:06:36.920 --> 00:06:39.680
unlike drug trials where
you can give people

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:43.120
a disguised placebo
sugar pill,

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:46.000
when a study involves major
diet and lifestyle changes,

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:49.280
you can’t rule out
the placebo effect,

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:52.320
especially for self-
reported, subjective

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:55.560
“How’s your memory been?”
type-questions.

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:57.720
But the researchers
also measured

00:06:57.720 --> 00:07:01.880
objective investigational biomarkers
of disease progression

00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:04.080
and saw the same
trajectory—

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:06.480
improvements in the
interventional group

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:09.400
and worsening in
the control group,

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:12.680
with the same apparent
dose-response effect,

00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:15.080
meaning the more they improved
their diet and lifestyle,

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:18.240
the more dramatic the effect.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:21.760
Compare that to the
latest Alzheimer’s drugs,

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:24.760
which may not
even work at all.

00:07:24.760 --> 00:07:29.120
All you may get
for your $56,000

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:35.280
is a one-in-three chance of
swelling or bleeding in your brain.

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:37.560
When the U.S. Food
and Drug Administration

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:39.640
approved the
drug anyway,

00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:43.800
the head of the American
Geriatrics Society replied,

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:47.240
“My head just exploded.”

00:07:47.240 --> 00:07:51.000
The bottom line is
there is only one diet

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:54.760
that's ever been shown to help
reverse our leading cause of death,

00:07:54.760 --> 00:07:57.240
heart disease,
in the majority of patients:

00:07:57.240 --> 00:07:59.205
a plant-based diet.

00:07:59.205 --> 00:08:01.800
If that’s all a plant-
based diet could do—

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:05.600
reverse the number one
killer of men and women,

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:09.360
then shouldn’t that be the default
diet until proven otherwise?

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:11.160
And the fact that it
can also be so effective

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:13.280
in preventing, arresting,
and reversing

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:15.480
the progression of
other leading killers,

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:18.200
like high blood pressure,
type 2 diabetes,

00:08:18.200 --> 00:08:21.160
and now maybe even early-
stage Alzheimer’s disease

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:23.480
would seem to make the
case for plant-based eating

00:08:23.480 --> 00:08:26.856
simply overwhelming.

